(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["anatomy-exam-2-integumentary-system-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"The Integumentary System — Anatomy Exam 2 — Set 2","category":"Anatomy","exam":"Exam 2","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"What is the cutaneous plexus, and where is it located?","choices":["A network of nerves found in the epidermis, transmitting temperature signals to the spinal cord","A network of sweat gland ducts draining directly into the papillary dermis","A lymphatic network found only in the dermis, unrelated to blood supply","An interconnected network of blood vessels found in the subcutaneous layer, just below the reticular layer"],"answer":3,"exp":"The cutaneous plexus is an interconnected network of blood vessels found in the subcutaneous layer, just below the reticular layer of the dermis; its branches supply blood to the lipid layer as well as the skin. — The Integumentary System - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"What is the papillary plexus?","choices":["A lymphatic vessel network in the hypodermis, draining fluid from the papillary dermis","A network of nerves that reach the stratum granulosum","Smaller branches of the cutaneous plexus that reach the epidermis","The main network of lymphatic vessels in the reticular layer"],"answer":2,"exp":"The papillary plexus consists of smaller branches from the dermal blood supply that reach the epidermis (supplying the papillary layer near the epidermal-dermal junction). — The Integumentary System - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"How does regulated skin blood supply contribute to thermoregulation?","choices":["Skin blood flow only decreases when body temperature increases, conserving heat regardless of the body's actual cooling needs","Increased skin blood flow always occurs alongside increased blood flow to all other organs simultaneously, regardless of total volume","When body temperature increases, increased skin circulation permits heat loss, and vice versa; increased blood flow to skin results in decreased blood flow to other organs","Skin blood flow has no relationship to body temperature regulation, remaining essentially constant across all environmental conditions"],"answer":2,"exp":"When body temperature increases, increased skin circulation permits heat loss (and vice versa when temperature decreases); increased blood flow to the skin results in decreased blood flow to other organs, as these organs interact to maintain constant total body blood flow. — The Integumentary System - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"What are the three general functions of the skin's nerve supply?","choices":["Only produce keratin, hardening the epidermal surface for protection","Only regulate hair growth, via direct innervation of the hair matrix","Only produce melanin, in response to sympathetic nerve stimulation","Control blood flow, adjust gland secretion, and monitor sensory receptors"],"answer":3,"exp":"The skin's nerve supply functions to control blood flow, adjust gland secretion, and monitor sensory receptors. — The Integumentary System - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"What do tactile corpuscles detect, and where are they located?","choices":["Light touch; located in dermal papillae and the root hair plexus","Deep pressure and vibration; located in the papillary layer","Stretch; located in the reticular layer, anchored to collagen bundles","Temperature; located in the epidermis only, near the stratum granulosum"],"answer":0,"exp":"Tactile corpuscles detect light touch and are located in the dermal papillae and root hair plexus. — The Integumentary System - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"What do Ruffini corpuscles detect, and where are they located within the skin?","choices":["Light touch; located in dermal papillae","Stretch; located in the reticular layer","Pain; located in the epidermis","Deep pressure and vibration; located in the reticular layer"],"answer":1,"exp":"Ruffini corpuscles detect stretch and are located in the reticular layer. — The Integumentary System - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"What do lamellated corpuscles detect, and where are they located?","choices":["Light touch; located in dermal papillae and the root hair plexus","Deep pressure and vibration; located in the reticular layer","Stretch; located in the papillary layer, adjacent to tactile corpuscles","Temperature only; located in the epidermis, near the stratum corneum"],"answer":1,"exp":"Lamellated corpuscles detect deep pressure and vibration and are located in the reticular layer. — The Integumentary System - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"What are the three main accessory structures of the integumentary system, and how do they develop embryonically?","choices":["Hair, glands, and nails, all developing from the dermis exclusively, with no epidermal contribution","Muscles, bones, and glands","Hair follicles/hair, glands, and nails; all develop through invagination/infolding of the epidermis","Only hair and nails, with no glands"],"answer":2,"exp":"The three main accessory structures are hair follicles and hair, glands, and nails; all of them developed embryonically through invagination/infolding of the epidermis. — The Integumentary System - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"What are the hair papillae, hair bulb, and hair matrix?","choices":["Hair papillae contain capillaries and nerves; hair bulb consists of epithelial cells; hair matrix is involved in hair production","Hair papillae are epithelial cells; hair bulb contains capillaries and nerves; hair matrix is unrelated to hair production","Hair papillae are epithelial cells; hair bulb is unrelated to hair growth; hair matrix contains capillaries and nerves","None of these structures relate to hair follicle anatomy, belonging instead to nail formation"],"answer":0,"exp":"The hair papillae contain capillaries and nerves, the hair bulb consists of epithelial cells, and the hair matrix is involved in hair production. — The Integumentary System - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"What are the three components of the hair shaft itself, and what type of keratin does each contain?","choices":["Only two components: cortex and cuticle","Medulla (soft keratin), cortex (hard keratin), and cuticle (hard keratin)","Medulla (hard keratin), cortex (soft keratin), and cuticle (soft keratin)","The hair shaft contains no keratin at all"],"answer":1,"exp":"Hair is composed of the medulla (containing soft keratin), the cortex (containing hard keratin), and the cuticle (containing hard keratin). — The Integumentary System - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"What are the three layers of the hair follicle, per the material?","choices":["Medulla, cortex, and cuticle, forming the layers of the hair shaft","Stratum basale, stratum spinosum, and stratum lucidum","Hair root, hair shaft, and hair cuticle","Internal root sheath, external root sheath, and glassy membrane"],"answer":3,"exp":"The hair follicle is organized into three layers: the internal root sheath (surrounds the hair root and part of the shaft), the external root sheath (extends from the skin surface to the matrix), and the glassy membrane (a thick basal lamina wrapped by dense connective tissue). — The Integumentary System - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"What is the function of hair at the nostrils, external ear canal, and eyelashes?","choices":["Serve as an early warning system to prevent injury","Protect the scalp from UV light","Cushion blows to the head","Prevent entry of foreign objects and insects"],"answer":3,"exp":"Hair at the nostrils, external ear canal, and eyelashes prevents the entry of foreign objects and insects. — The Integumentary System - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"What are the three types of hair described in the material?","choices":["Cortex, cuticle, and follicle","Only vellus and terminal hair","Vellus, intermediate, and terminal","Only intermediate hair types"],"answer":2,"exp":"The three types of hair are vellus, intermediate, and terminal. — The Integumentary System - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"What do sebaceous glands produce, and where are sebaceous follicles specifically found?","choices":["Produce a clear, watery secretion; found everywhere on the body's surface except the palms and soles of the hands and feet","Produce sweat for thermoregulation; found only in the axillae, groin, and surrounding apocrine-rich regions","Produce oily lipid (waxy/oily) coating hair and epidermis; sebaceous follicles found only in the face, back, chest, nipples, and male sex organs","Produce keratin; found only in the thick skin of the palms, soles, and fingertip pads of the hands and feet"],"answer":2,"exp":"Sebaceous glands produce an oily lipid (waxy and oily) that coats the hair (typical glands) and epidermis (sebaceous follicles); sebaceous follicles specifically are found only in the face, back, chest, nipples, and male sex organs. — The Integumentary System - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"What is the difference between apocrine and merocrine sweat glands, in terms of location?","choices":["Apocrine glands are confined to the scalp and face; merocrine glands are found in the axillae only","Neither gland type is found on the skin's surface or within its glandular structures","Apocrine glands are found in the axillae, groin, and nipples only; merocrine glands are found everywhere else","Both gland types are found in identical locations across the entire body surface"],"answer":2,"exp":"Apocrine sweat glands are found in the axillae, groin, and nipples only, while merocrine sweat glands are found everywhere else on the body. — The Integumentary System - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"What do myoepithelial cells do in sweat glands?","choices":["Produce keratin","Produce melanin pigment","Constrict the gland to discharge secretions","Form the sensory nerve endings of the gland"],"answer":2,"exp":"Sweat glands contain myoepithelial cells, which constrict the gland to discharge secretions. — The Integumentary System - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"What are the functions of sweat, per the material?","choices":["Digestion of food particles on the skin surface","Thermoregulation, excretion, and protection","Only thermoregulation","Only excretion of waste"],"answer":1,"exp":"The functions of sweat are thermoregulation, excretion, and protection. — The Integumentary System - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"How is control of sebaceous/apocrine gland secretion different from control of merocrine gland secretion?","choices":["Both gland categories have identical control precision, since all are governed uniformly by the hypothalamus","Neither gland category is controlled by the autonomic nervous system, relying instead on local reflexes","Sebaceous/apocrine glands respond only to temperature; merocrine glands respond only to emotional stress","For sebaceous and apocrine glands, the autonomic control is general and regional control is impossible; for merocrine glands, control is more precise"],"answer":3,"exp":"Secretions of exocrine glands are controlled by the autonomic nervous system; for sebaceous and apocrine glands, the effect is general and regional control is impossible, while for merocrine glands, the control is more precise. — The Integumentary System - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"What are mammary glands related to, and what are ceruminous glands?","choices":["Mammary glands are related to apocrine glands; ceruminous glands are modified sweat glands in the external ear canal containing pigmented granules and lipid droplets","Neither gland type is related to the integumentary system, belonging instead to the endocrine system","Mammary and ceruminous glands are actually the same structure, differing only in their anatomical location and terminology","Mammary glands are related to merocrine glands; ceruminous glands are found in the eyes, not the ear canal as commonly assumed"],"answer":0,"exp":"Mammary glands are related to apocrine glands, and ceruminous glands are modified sweat glands located in the external ear canals, containing pigmented granules and lipid droplets. — The Integumentary System - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"What is the difference between the nail body/plate and the nail bed?","choices":["Both terms describe the identical structure, differing only in the clinical terminology used","Nail body/plate is the external component, bounded by nail grooves and folds; nail bed is the internal component","Nail body/plate is the internal component; nail bed is the external, visible component","Neither structure is part of the nail anatomy, belonging instead to the surrounding cuticle"],"answer":1,"exp":"The nail body/plate is the external component, bounded by nail grooves and folds, while the nail bed is the internal component. — The Integumentary System - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"What are the eponychium and hyponychium?","choices":["Eponychium is the lateral nail fold along the sides; hyponychium is the pale lunula near the nail root","Eponychium extends over the nail body at the nail root; hyponychium is the free end of the nail bed","Both terms describe the nail root itself, differing only in embryonic origin","Neither structure is associated with the nail, belonging instead to the surrounding dermis"],"answer":1,"exp":"The eponychium extends over the nail body at the nail root, while the hyponychium is the free end of the nail bed. — The Integumentary System - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"Where does the nail root lie, anatomically?","choices":["Within the subcutaneous fat layer only, well below the level of the periosteum","Within the dermis's reticular layer only, anchored by collagen fibers alone","Entirely within the hypodermis, with no connection to bone","Close to the periosteum of the underlying bone, as an epithelial fold"],"answer":3,"exp":"The nail root, an epithelial fold, lies close to the periosteum of the underlying bone. — The Integumentary System - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"Why do lacerations or incisions that parallel tension (cleavage) lines usually heal well?","choices":["Because tension lines are located only in the epidermis","Because tension lines contain no collagen fibers at all","Because there is minimal disruption of the collagen fibers","Because parallel incisions always avoid nerve damage"],"answer":2,"exp":"Lacerations or surgical incisions that parallel the tension lines usually heal well because there is minimal disruption of the collagen fibers. — The Integumentary System - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"What happens when a laceration or incision is made across (perpendicular to) tension lines?","choices":["The collagen fibers are undisturbed, remaining in their original parallel orientation","The wound heals faster with minimal scarring, since tension is evenly distributed","Greater disruption to collagen fibers, causing the wound to gape and heal with excessive scarring","No difference compared to a parallel incision, since collagen orientation doesn't affect healing"],"answer":2,"exp":"When a laceration or incision is made across (or perpendicular to) the tension lines, there is greater disruption to the collagen fibers, causing the wound to gape and heal with excessive scarring. — The Integumentary System - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"What causes stretch marks, and what color changes do they undergo?","choices":["Caused by excessive sun exposure damaging the epidermis; the resulting bands turn from white to red only, then gradually fade","Damage to the epidermis only, with no dermal involvement at all; the resulting bands remain a constant, unchanging color","Caused by bacterial infection of the dermal layer; the resulting bands turn from purple to white only, over several weeks","Damage to collagen fibers in the dermis from rapid/excessive enlargement (e.g., pregnancy); bands of skin appear initially red, become purple, then finally white"],"answer":3,"exp":"Stretch marks result from damage to the collagen fibers in the dermis when an area (like the abdomen during pregnancy) enlarges too large or too fast; the resulting bands of thin, wrinkled skin initially appear red, become purple later, and then finally turn white. — The Integumentary System - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"Do stretch marks disappear completely after pregnancy and weight loss?","choices":["They only appear after pregnancy, never fading","They disappear completely and immediately","Generally they fade but don't disappear completely","They never fade at all, remaining exactly as they first appeared"],"answer":2,"exp":"Stretch marks generally fade, but don't disappear completely, after pregnancy and weight loss. — The Integumentary System - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"What is a superficial burn, per the classification given?","choices":["Damage to the superficial part of the epidermis only","Damage to the entire epidermis and dermis, possibly including muscle","Damage extending into the dermis, sparing sweat glands and hair follicles","Damage limited to the subcutaneous fat layer only"],"answer":0,"exp":"A superficial burn involves damage to the superficial part of the epidermis only. — The Integumentary System - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"What is a partial thickness burn, and are sweat glands/hair follicles damaged?","choices":["Damage affects the entire epidermis, dermis, and subcutaneous fat; sweat glands and hair follicles are destroyed","Damage affects the epidermis into the superficial part of the dermis; sweat glands and hair follicles are not damaged","Damage is confined to the epidermis only, with no dermal involvement; sweat glands and hair follicles are destroyed","Damage extends through the epidermis into the deep reticular dermis; sweat glands and hair follicles are destroyed"],"answer":1,"exp":"A partial thickness burn affects the epidermis into the superficial part of the dermis, but sweat glands and hair follicles are not damaged. — The Integumentary System - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"What is a full thickness burn?","choices":["Damage affecting only the superficial dermis, sparing the epidermis","Damage that never affects sweat glands or hair follicles","Damage limited to the superficial epidermis only","The entire epidermis and dermis (possibly even muscle) are affected"],"answer":3,"exp":"A full thickness burn affects the entire epidermis and dermis, and may even extend to affect muscle. — The Integumentary System - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"What structures does the deep layer of the dermis contain?","choices":["Only lymphatic capillaries, with no glandular or hair structures","The stratum corneum, forming the deepest layer of dead, keratinized cells","Hair follicles with their associated arrector pili muscles and sebaceous glands","Only melanocytes, scattered evenly throughout the deep reticular fibers"],"answer":2,"exp":"The deep layer of the dermis contains hair follicles, with their associated smooth arrector muscles (arrector pili) and sebaceous glands. — The Integumentary System - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 27"}]};
